1. If a model's loss is increasing over time instead of decreasing what might be wrong with the learning rate?
The learning rate might be too hard. 

2. Why is it important to call optimizer.zero_grad() at the beginning of each training step?
It needs to clear the old gradients so that each training step can start anew and not snowball previous gradients. 

3. Which loss function would you choose if you were building a model to predict the temperature for tomorrow?
Adam because it automatically adjusts the learning rate as it trains. 

4. What is the difference between loss.backward() and optimizer.step()?
loss.backward measures the difference between the target and the predictions whereas the optimizer.step updates the weights. 

5. Why should you use loss.item() instead of just loss when apppending to a list of metrics?
loss.item() is specific and will save exactly what you will request but if you do just loss then it will take the entire computational graph in memory which will crash the computer.
